course: language
lesson: 35
title: French compared with English
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: French is English's closest neighbour in words: after 1066 French-speaking rulers poured thousands of words into English, so when you learn French you already know a lot of it.
explain: But French does some things its own way: Zoe learns "la table" is feminine and "le livre" is masculine, and wonders if tables wear dresses; they don't, it's just a grammar label.
explain: French counts some numbers in twenties: 80 is "quatre-vingts", four twenties, like the old English "three score and ten" for 70.
explain: Watch out for false friends, words that look English but mean something else: ask a French baker for "pain" and you get bread, not hurt.
explain: French also has many varieties around the world, from Paris to Quebec to West Africa, and none of them is "the only correct French".
fact: French nouns have grammatical gender: masculine with "le" and feminine with "la"; it is a grammar label, not about real boys and girls; English lost gender for nouns long ago.
fact: 70 in French is "soixante-dix" (sixty-ten), 80 is "quatre-vingts" (four twenties) and 90 is "quatre-vingt-dix" (four twenties ten); in Belgium and Switzerland people say "septante" for 70 and "nonante" for 90.
fact: False friends look like English words but mean something different: "pain" means bread, "coin" means corner, "librairie" means bookshop and "raisin" means grape.
fact: Most French adjectives come after the noun: "une voiture rouge" is "a red car", word for word "a car red"; a few common ones like "petit" (small) and "grand" (big) come before.
fact: French "tu" is the informal you, for friends and family, and "vous" is the polite or plural you, just like Ukrainian "ти" and "ви".
fact: Many French endings are written but not said: "ils parlent" (they speak) sounds like "eel parl".
fact: English uses whole French phrases: "rendezvous" (a meeting), "déjà vu" (already seen, the odd feeling of having lived a moment before) and "bon appétit" (enjoy your meal).
fact: In Quebec, in Canada, "C'est l'fun!" means "It's fun!"; Quebec French has its own words, accents and expressions, like the different kinds of English.
fact: French uses "to have" for some feelings and age: "j'ai faim" (I'm hungry), "j'ai froid" (I'm cold) and "j'ai dix ans" (I'm ten).
fact: After 1066 many English words for law, the army and cooking came from French, like "jury", "lieutenant" and "chef".
exercise: Zoe learns "la table" is feminine. Is a table a girl? | No, it's a grammar label | Yes, all tables are girls | Only round tables | Only in Paris
exercise: Which little word goes with a masculine French noun? | Le | La | Les | Lu
exercise: What is 70 in French, word for word? | Sixty-ten | Seven-ten | Seventy | Fifty-twenty
exercise: What does "quatre-vingts" (80) mean word for word? | Four twenties | Eight tens | Four tens | Twenty-four times
exercise: What do people in Belgium and Switzerland say for 70? | Septante | Soixante-dix | Quatre-vingts | Nonante
exercise: Mia asks for "pain" in a French bakery. What will she get? | Bread | Medicine | Nothing | A cake
exercise: What does French "coin" mean? | Corner | Money | Coin | Cow
exercise: What is a false friend? | A word that looks English but means something else | A friend who tells lies about you behind your back | A word spelled backwards | A word borrowed exactly
exercise: Ben learns "une voiture rouge". Where does the adjective usually go in French? | After the noun | Before the noun | At the end of the sentence | At the start of the sentence
exercise: Freya learns "tu" and "vous". Which Ukrainian word is like "vous"? | Ви | Ти | Я | Ми
exercise: Max says "ils parlent" as "eel par-LENT". What's right? | The "-ent" is silent: "eel parl" | He said it perfectly, every letter is sounded | Every letter is said loudly | The "ils" is silent
exercise: What does "déjà vu" mean word for word? | Already seen | Good food | A meeting | See you later
exercise: A boy from Quebec says "C'est l'fun!" What does he mean? | It's fun | It's funny-looking | It's finished | It's a fan
exercise: Sam's French friend says "J'ai faim". What does it mean? | I'm hungry | I'm famous | I'm tired | I'm cold
exercise: How do you say "I'm ten" in French? | J'ai dix ans | Je suis dix | J'ai faim | Je dix ans
exercise: Josh learns "jury" and "lieutenant" come from French. Why are there so many French words in English law and the army? | French-speaking Normans ruled England after 1066 | French was invented in England by lawyers and soldiers | English copied French TV | Nobody knows at all
test: Your mate goes "French numbers are mad nonsense, sixty-ten, lol." What's fairer? | It's a different system counting in twenties, not a wrong one | He's right, French people just can't count properly past sixty | French has no number 70 | It's a spelling mistake
test: A Paris tourist laughs at a Quebec boy saying "C'est l'fun". What's true? | Quebec French is a real variety with its own expressions | Only Paris French is correct, and Quebec French is a mistake | Quebec people speak English, not French | It's a typo
test: What would happen if you told a French baker "I'd like some pain" in English, and she only knew French? | She might think you want bread | She'd call a doctor | She'd sell you a coin | She'd understand you're hurt
test: Your gran from Somerset says "gurt lush, that French bread, proper job." ("Gurt" means very, "lush" means lovely.) Which French word means bread? | Pain | Coin | Raisin | Librairie
test: Your cousin looks for a "librairie" in Paris to borrow a book for free. What will he find? | A bookshop | A library | A pharmacy | A bakery
test: A Gen Z teen says "I'm lowkey having déjà vu, this lesson is giving yesterday." ("It's giving" means it seems like.) What does déjà vu mean? | The feeling you've already lived this moment | Feeling so bored that time seems to stop | Feeling hungry | Seeing something new
test: Your mate writes "un rouge voiture" for "a red car". What would a French person say? | Une voiture rouge | Une rouge voiture | Le rouge voiture | Rouge une voiture
test: A cartoon French kid says "Je suis faim!" for "I'm hungry". What's the usual French? | J'ai faim | Je suis faim | J'ai froid | Je faim
test: Your brother says "English has only like five French words." What's true? | English has many French words, especially in food, law and the army | He's right, only five, and they're all the names of foods like croissant | English has no French words | All English words are French
test: Max's mate says "only English has silent letters, French says everything." What shows he's wrong? | "Ils parlent" sounds like "eel parl" | French has no letters | French says every letter twice | English has no silent letters
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises grammatical gender in French? | A classification of nouns as masculine or feminine for grammatical purposes, unrelated to biological sex | An indication that each object denoted by a noun is, in reality, either male or female in nature | A feature shared with English nouns in the present day | A feature confined to the names of animals
exam: Identify the literal sense of the French numeral "quatre-vingts". | Four twenties | Eight tens | Four tens | Forty-eight
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the French word "librairie". | It is a false friend signifying a bookshop | It signifies a public library, as the English form suggests | It signifies a laboratory | It signifies a bakery
exam: Ascertain the French expression signifying "I am hungry". | J'ai faim | Je suis faim | J'ai froid | Je suis fini
